we can talk now  to somebody who i think can tell us a  little bit more about what it is uh like  uh to be a target of uh russia and  whether the uh sanctions are gonna work  bill bralda has campaigned around the  world for sanctions on the putin regime  he's the author of freezing order and he  joins us now i just give a bit of  background to  i guess your own history with  russia  i was the largest foreign investor in  russia from 1995 to 2005 and i um  exposed corruption and the companies i  invested in  in retaliation i was expelled from the  country declared a threat to national  security  my offices in moscow were raided i had a  young lawyer named sergey magnitsky  investigate the reason for the office  raid he discovered that  the purpose was for these  corrupt police officers and other  officials to steal  230 million dollars of taxes that we  paid to the russian government  he exposed it testified against the  officials and he was subsequently  arrested  tortured for 358 days and murdered  in russian police custody  on november 16 2009  since then i've been on a mission to get  justice for sergey  and which is culminated in something  called the magnitsky act the magnitsky  act freezes the assets and bans the  visas  of the people who did that and people  who commit similar human rights abuses  in russia and around the world the  magnitsky act now exists in 34 countries  and is the template which is being used  to sanction oligarchs and putin cronies  in this war on ukraine  and so from one individual murder we now  have one of the most prolific tools to  deal with this terrible atrocity which  is going on every day in ukraine  okay i'm interested to know on your view  on the sanctions that the west have  imposed on  russia do you think they're effective  and if they're not what more could be  done  well the way i view sanctions is kind of  like medicine for disease it depends on  at which stage of the disease you've  taken the medicine  so had we started to sanction just a few  oligarchs let's say five before  um russia or putin i should say invaded  ukraine um he would have seen that we  had the capacity to do this he i think  his calculation was that we were never  going to do this type of sanctioning  because over the last 22 years he's done  a lot of other terrible things terrible  atrocities and we've always looked the  other way he invaded georgia nothing  crimea taking crimea nothing salisbury  nothing  um and many many other things nothing  and so he he was sort of under the  impression that this this was uh  uh gonna be the same thing with ukraine  had we just done a bit of the  sanctioning he might have changed his  calculus now that he's  invaded putin doesn't have a reverse  gear he doesn't have any capacity to  compromise to back down to show weakness  and so the purpose of the sanctions at  this point is not to change his mind  i don't think the sanctions will change  the oligarchs minds either because they  don't have any effect on on putin they  can't  they're they're subordinate to him  the effect the purpose of sanctions at  this point in my opinion is that we want  to starve putin  uh of all the financial resources  that he has to wage this war if we can  cut off his money in every different way  including by sanctioning the oligarchs  and sanctioning the russian government  etc um then perhaps in the future he's  not going to have the money to do what  he's he's been doing and plans to do  when we spoke to oliver dowden a little  bit earlier in the program he's a member  of the government here in the united  kingdom he said that he would like to  see germany and france doing more  is there a question that when some  european countries are still pumping  billions of pounds into russia in return  for oil and gas that actually the  sanctions are going to only have a  limited impact  well he's pointed out the elephant in  the room so we've done uh more  sanctioning of russia than  than anyone has done of any country in  history um freezing the central bank  reserves um freezing the oligarchs  cutting off russian banks from swift but  he's right and as you say every day  um uh europe germany france italy  austria various other places who are  totally dependent on russian gas  send vladimir putin a billion dollars  every day  and so just to put in really simple  terms  um this war costs vladimir putin a  billion dollars a day and every day he  gets a billion dollars from europe and  so that's kind of a wash  and so if we really do want to um stop  this war and we want to stop it from  spreading which is i think the biggest  concern we should all have  then we have to stop giving them the  money and to stop giving them the money  that means that um germany is going to  have to tighten their belt significantly  they're going to have to experience some  economic pain  and and they can say well this is  disproportionate you know what about the  uk which only gets three percent of  their gas  well i would argue that germany has put  themselves in this position they very  stridently argued um with the rest of  the world to become more dependent on  russian gas they built a pipeline to  become more dependent on russian gas and  a pipeline that was supposed to bypass  and effectively squeeze ukraine  economically and so i think that germany  has a big duty here i completely agree  that that um uh germany is sort of  sitting sitting in a very um  hypocritical seat right now  they're they're closer to the front line  than we are and and they're continuing  to to give putin money to pay for  weapons to kill ukrainians and  potentially kill other people in europe

